WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:02.268
-Our first guest tonight i
an Academy Award-nominated

00:00:02.268 --> 00:00:04.504 align:start size:94% position:6%
and Emmy Award-winning actress
you know from her work in

00:00:04.504 --> 00:00:06.106 align:start size:94% position:6%
the "The Wizard of Lies,"
"Dangerous Liaisons"

00:00:06.106 --> 00:00:06.806 size:78% position:39% line:92%
and "Batman Returns."

00:00:06.806 --> 00:00:11.511
She just received a Golden Globe
nomination for Best Actres

00:00:11.511 --> 00:00:12.946 line:92%
for her role in "French Exit,"

00:00:12.946 --> 00:00:15.382 align:start size:94% position:6%
which is in theaters
on February 12th.

00:00:15.382 --> 00:00:16.049 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Let's take a look.

00:00:16.049 --> 00:00:21.254
-I spoke to you about this as
possibility for seven years,

00:00:21.254 --> 00:00:23.923 align:start size:94% position:6%
and then an
eventuality for three.

00:00:23.923 --> 00:00:26.793 align:start size:94% position:6%
What did you think
was going to happen?

00:00:26.793 --> 00:00:31.297 align:start size:94% position:6%
Frances, what -- what
was your plan?

00:00:31.297 --> 00:00:34.067 align:start size:94% position:6%
-My plan was to die
before the money ran out,

00:00:34.067 --> 00:00:39.939 align:start size:94% position:6%
but I kept and keep not dying,
and...here I am.

00:00:39.939 --> 00:00:42.042 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Please welcome to the sho
Michelle Pfeiffer.

00:00:42.042 --> 00:00:44.344 align:start size:94% position:6%
Hello, Michelle.
Thanks for being here.

00:00:44.344 --> 00:00:45.445 line:92%
-Hi. Thanks for having me.

00:00:45.445 --> 00:00:49.883 align:start size:94% position:6%
-So, I want to ask -- You know
a lot of actors, you know,

00:00:49.883 --> 00:00:52.819
couldn't do a great deal of work
this last year,

00:00:52.819 --> 00:00:53.620 line:92%
when everything shut down,

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:57.357 align:start size:94% position:6%
but you also have
a fragrance company.

00:00:57.357 --> 00:00:58.825 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I'm wondering if you
were able to sort of

00:00:58.825 --> 00:01:03.696 align:start size:94% position:6%
throw yourself into that as so
much else was unavailable.

00:01:03.696 --> 00:01:06.766 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I feel really lucky
that not only

00:01:06.766 --> 00:01:12.939 align:start size:94% position:6%
did my fragrance company -
henryrose.com --

00:01:12.939 --> 00:01:16.609 align:start size:94% position:6%
not only did it survive
this past year,

00:01:16.609 --> 00:01:21.414 align:start size:94% position:6%
but, yes, I had
something to, you know,

00:01:21.414 --> 00:01:24.017 size:88% position:44% line:92%
get up and do every day.

00:01:24.017 --> 00:01:28.388
And it was great, because it
really gave me an opportunit

00:01:28.388 --> 00:01:34.094 align:start size:94% position:6%
to really get hyper-focuse
on the business.

00:01:34.094 --> 00:01:35.895 line:92%
-And do you also have a very -

00:01:35.895 --> 00:01:37.363 align:start size:94% position:6%
Do you feel like
you are someone who has

00:01:37.363 --> 00:01:39.032 line:92%
a very good nose for fragrance

00:01:39.032 --> 00:01:39.866 line:92%
Because it would strike me

00:01:39.866 --> 00:01:43.002 align:start size:94% position:6%
that that's an
important element of it.

00:01:43.002 --> 00:01:45.438 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-I actually do.

00:01:45.438 --> 00:01:46.239 line:92%
But I had to learn, though

00:01:46.239 --> 00:01:50.176 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, the sort of language
of a perfumer

00:01:50.176 --> 00:01:54.180 align:start size:94% position:6%
is very different,
and I would start with say--

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:57.317
You know, I would start with
saying, "Oh, I don't know.

00:01:57.317 --> 00:01:59.686 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think it smells
a little bit orange."

00:01:59.686 --> 00:02:00.653 line:92%
And they would say, "No, no, no.

00:02:00.653 --> 00:02:02.021 align:start size:94% position:6%
Don't tell me what
you think it smells like.

00:02:02.021 --> 00:02:08.428 align:start size:94% position:6%
Tell me what you --
Tell me what it reminds you of

00:02:08.428 --> 00:02:11.197 align:start size:94% position:6%
What do you think
of when you smell it?"

00:02:11.197 --> 00:02:14.434 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I learned to
say things like...

00:02:14.434 --> 00:02:16.970 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Sniffs ]
"Oh, I don't know.

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:21.341 align:start size:94% position:6%
It smells like...
baby-doll head."

00:02:21.341 --> 00:02:26.579 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so, you know,
I've learned how

00:02:26.579 --> 00:02:27.547 line:92%
to sort of speak their language.

00:02:27.547 --> 00:02:32.919
And, as it turns out, I actually
do have a pretty good nose

00:02:32.919 --> 00:02:33.987 align:start size:94% position:6%
Good thing.
-Yeah.

00:02:33.987 --> 00:02:36.156 align:start size:94% position:6%
I imagine if you were, like,
congested all the time,

00:02:36.156 --> 00:02:38.691 align:start size:94% position:6%
you should probably
find a different gig.

00:02:38.691 --> 00:02:41.227 align:start size:94% position:6%
This movie is really wonderful
and it seems like

00:02:41.227 --> 00:02:44.531 align:start size:94% position:6%
maybe something we haven't
seen from you before.

00:02:44.531 --> 00:02:46.566 align:start size:94% position:6%
As the clip maybe
explains pretty well,

00:02:46.566 --> 00:02:48.701 size:78% position:39%
you play a socialite
who runs out of money

00:02:48.701 --> 00:02:50.937 align:start size:94% position:6%
and then leaves
New York for Paris.

00:02:50.937 --> 00:02:55.642
Did you know right away when you
were reading it how good it was?

00:02:55.642 --> 00:02:56.109 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-I did.

00:02:56.109 --> 00:02:58.244 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, you know,
that doesn't happen every --

00:02:58.244 --> 00:03:00.246 line:92%
That doesn't happen very often

00:03:00.246 --> 00:03:05.852 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I -- Patrick deWitt
is such a wonderful writer

00:03:05.852 --> 00:03:09.455 line:92%
and he has such a unique voice

00:03:09.455 --> 00:03:13.526 size:84% position:42%
And he writes with --
You know, he sort of --

00:03:13.526 --> 00:03:16.429 align:start size:94% position:6%
All of his characters
use comedy as a coping tool,

00:03:16.429 --> 00:03:22.202 align:start size:94% position:6%
and they're sort of dark
and funny and absurd

00:03:22.202 --> 00:03:27.607
and, yet, you know, these craz
sort of eccentric characters

00:03:27.607 --> 00:03:29.108 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, yet,
somehow by the end of it,

00:03:29.108 --> 00:03:31.578 align:start size:94% position:6%
you end up being able to
relate to them

00:03:31.578 --> 00:03:35.481 align:start size:94% position:6%
and seeing yourselves
in their --

00:03:35.481 --> 00:03:36.349 line:92%
you know, in their journey

00:03:36.349 --> 00:03:39.419 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I just --
And I loved this character

00:03:39.419 --> 00:03:41.421 line:92%
She was unlike anything I ha

00:03:41.421 --> 00:03:45.925 size:84% position:42%
ever read or, you know,
anyone I've ever met.

00:03:45.925 --> 00:03:52.098
Very complicated, very kind of
takes-no-prisoners attitude,

00:03:52.098 --> 00:03:59.973 align:start size:94% position:6%
and, you know, a bit, kind o
coarse and curt at times,

00:03:59.973 --> 00:04:03.610 align:start size:94% position:6%
but, underneath it,
a kind of a fragility.

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:07.880 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it was, you know,
a lot of fun to play somebod

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:09.582 align:start size:94% position:6%
who just speaks their mind
all the time.

00:04:09.582 --> 00:04:12.185
-I did read you were a littl
scared about taking on the role.

00:04:12.185 --> 00:04:15.888 align:start size:94% position:6%
What was it that gave you
any hesitation?

00:04:15.888 --> 00:04:21.060
-Well, I think there's som
parts that are closer to you

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:25.531 line:92%
and this one wasn't at all

00:04:25.531 --> 00:04:32.905
And she was from a world tha
I wasn't that familiar with.

00:04:32.905 --> 00:04:38.945 align:start size:94% position:6%
I had some friends, I had
sort of brushes with this worl

00:04:38.945 --> 00:04:43.616 align:start size:94% position:6%
and this kind of woman
throughout my life,

00:04:43.616 --> 00:04:47.987 align:start size:94% position:6%
because I had friends
who grew up in this world.

00:04:47.987 --> 00:04:50.923 line:92%
But -- And I was also kind of --

00:04:50.923 --> 00:04:52.859 align:start size:94% position:6%
I knew that it was going t
take a lot out of me,

00:04:52.859 --> 00:04:56.362 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I was a little bit
depleted when I started it

00:04:56.362 --> 00:04:58.231 align:start size:94% position:6%
But the thing about
great material is,

00:04:58.231 --> 00:05:01.634 align:start size:94% position:6%
it kind of just infuses
you every day,

00:05:01.634 --> 00:05:04.337 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you don't --
It just sort of --

00:05:04.337 --> 00:05:07.774
You wake up every morning, and
it kind of carries you through

00:05:07.774 --> 00:05:12.011 align:start size:94% position:6%
And we had such an amazing cas
in working with --

00:05:12.011 --> 00:05:15.815 align:start size:94% position:6%
Aza Jacobs was incredible
and Lucas Hedges

00:05:15.815 --> 00:05:20.386 size:78% position:39% line:92%
and Valerie Mahaffey.

00:05:20.386 --> 00:05:23.122 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Mahaffey?

00:05:23.122 --> 00:05:26.592 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, it was just --
It was a real joy.

00:05:26.592 --> 00:05:29.595
-You -- I'm not the first person
to make an observation

00:05:29.595 --> 00:05:33.433 align:start size:94% position:6%
that there is a character
in this film that is

00:05:33.433 --> 00:05:34.200 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
a cat, a black cat.

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:38.438 align:start size:94% position:6%
You famously played -- and
fantastically, I should note -

00:05:38.438 --> 00:05:38.971 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
played Catwoman.

00:05:38.971 --> 00:05:44.577 align:start size:94% position:6%
Was the cat community excite
to work with you again?

00:05:44.577 --> 00:05:46.713 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Thrilled.
-Yes.

00:05:46.713 --> 00:05:48.147 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Yes. Yeah.

00:05:48.147 --> 00:05:49.682 line:92%
You know, and I didn't really --

00:05:49.682 --> 00:05:52.285 align:start size:94% position:6%
I know -- I have this kind o
running theme,

00:05:52.285 --> 00:05:56.122 align:start size:94% position:6%
and didn't sort of even
realize it until, actually

00:05:56.122 --> 00:05:59.592 align:start size:94% position:6%
journalists started
asking me about it.

00:05:59.592 --> 00:06:01.627 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I do love cats.
I have a cat.

00:06:01.627 --> 00:06:05.531 align:start size:94% position:6%
She's not as well behaved
as the cats that we work with.

00:06:05.531 --> 00:06:09.469 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was -- One of my
first questions for Aza was,

00:06:09.469 --> 00:06:11.971 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Number one, talk to
me about the tone of this,

00:06:11.971 --> 00:06:14.774 align:start size:94% position:6%
because I felt like
that was critical.

00:06:14.774 --> 00:06:16.242 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then
my second question was,

00:06:16.242 --> 00:06:18.277 line:92%
"Okay, what about this cat

00:06:18.277 --> 00:06:22.281 align:start size:94% position:6%
And how are you going to
deal with that?"

00:06:22.281 --> 00:06:26.319 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it's so
funny and fascinating

00:06:26.319 --> 00:06:31.924
that the characters in the piece
just find it completely normal

00:06:31.924 --> 00:06:33.092 line:92%
that there's a talking cat

00:06:33.092 --> 00:06:35.862 align:start size:94% position:6%
-How many cats do you thin
you worked with

00:06:35.862 --> 00:06:40.400 align:start size:94% position:6%
to play the one cat
in the movie?

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:43.469 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Primarily just two.
-Okay.

00:06:43.469 --> 00:06:46.406 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And they were
amazing and really sweet

00:06:46.406 --> 00:06:51.411
and just kind of normal,
you know, not standoffish.

00:06:51.411 --> 00:06:53.479 size:78% position:39% line:92%
And they were lovely.

00:06:53.479 --> 00:06:56.749 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I guess there was
another one that I didn't know

00:06:56.749 --> 00:07:00.720 align:start size:94% position:6%
until recently
that was a tiny bit edgier

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:02.889 line:92%
I'm not going to say mean.

00:07:02.889 --> 00:07:06.959 align:start size:94% position:6%
But they only brought
that cat out in one scene,

00:07:06.959 --> 00:07:12.498 size:81% position:41%
where there's a bit of
a confrontation with

00:07:12.498 --> 00:07:14.934 size:88% position:44% line:92%
Frances and Small Frank.

00:07:14.934 --> 00:07:18.538 align:start size:94% position:6%
-So it was sort of the
emotional tenor of that scen

00:07:18.538 --> 00:07:19.872 size:75% position:38% line:92%
called for that cat.

00:07:19.872 --> 00:07:20.540 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Exactly.
-Wow.

00:07:20.540 --> 00:07:26.212 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, you know, as with animals
they have numerous animals

00:07:26.212 --> 00:07:27.547 size:81% position:41% line:92%
to play the same part,

00:07:27.547 --> 00:07:33.186 align:start size:94% position:6%
and they are usually
better at different things

00:07:33.186 --> 00:07:35.488 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, I thought
they were all great,

00:07:35.488 --> 00:07:38.858 align:start size:94% position:6%
but all paled
in comparison to you.

00:07:38.858 --> 00:07:41.994
Thank you so much for making the
time and being with us today

00:07:41.994 --> 00:07:45.898 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I'm so happy.
Nice to be on the show.

00:07:45.898 --> 00:07:47.733 align:start size:94% position:6%
-"French Exit"
will be in theaters

00:07:47.733 --> 00:07:49.535 line:92%
on Friday, February 12th.

